// Package helptopics holds the short guides that ship inside the binary. The command line prints them as help
// topics.
package helptopics

import "github.com/castrowithcee/qatlas-kit/helptopics"

// All returns the topics in the order a newcomer reads them. The text is plain ASCII with one line per
// paragraph, list item, or command; the kit fits it to the width where it is shown.
func All() []helptopics.Topic {
	return []helptopics.Topic{
		{Name: "qhost", Short: "What qhost is and what it is for", Text: overview},
		{Name: "deploy-setup", Short: "The deploy service as its own service, and an instance per rootless engine: what the setup creates and why", Text: deployServiceTopic},
		{Name: "hook-setup", Short: "The webhook receiver as its own service: setup, address, port, HTTPS through a reverse proxy, and what it may and may not do", Text: hookServiceTopic},
		{Name: "credentials", Short: "The deploy keys and stored credentials", Text: credentialsTopic},
		{Name: "repository", Short: "Binding the stacks repository and trusting its host key", Text: repositoryTopic},
		{Name: "sources", Short: "Several stack folders on one host: taking over a folder, ids, deploy keys and unique stack names", Text: sourcesTopic},
		{Name: "stack-capabilities", Short: "What qhost can do with a stack's files, and what qhost stack check reports", Text: stackCapabilitiesTopic},
		{Name: "stack-settings", Short: "Stack settings", Text: stackSettingsTopic},
		{Name: "backups", Short: "Backing up and restoring the data of a stack", Text: backupsTopic},
		{Name: "data-directories", Short: "Missing data directories of a stack: the data-dir-missing finding and how to resolve it", Text: dataDirectoriesTopic},
		{Name: "notifications", Short: "Notification channels: events, types and secrets", Text: notificationsTopic},
		{Name: "proposals", Short: "Proposals: asking for a change of the setup or the host without sudo, the kinds source-add, stack-settings, window, notify-channel, host-change and the switched command, limits and states", Text: proposalsTopic},
		{Name: "containers", Short: "The containers of the host: kinds, states, metrics, start, stop, restart", Text: containersTopic},
		{Name: "host-admin", Short: "Read and change the users, groups and folder permissions of the host", Text: hostAdminTopic},
		{Name: "troubleshooting", Short: "A state file that qhost cannot read: what the message means and what to do", Text: troubleshootingTopic},
		{Name: "host-tui", Short: "The area System of the terminal interface: the users, groups and folder permissions of the host, its latest changes with undo and the action log", Text: hostTUITopic},
	}
}

const overview = `qhost runs as a service on a Linux host and keeps the Docker stacks of that host rolled out and up to date from its stacks repository.

Commands:
- qhost run: run the host service in the foreground until it receives SIGTERM or SIGINT; it serves root and the administrators of the group qhost, checks the administrator right in /etc/group on every request and keeps the action log; the deploy service is started by its own unit with qhost deploy-service run
- qhost deploy-service setup [--rootless <user>] [--confirm] [--yes]: set up the deploy service as its own system user qhost-deploy with the group docker, or with --rootless an instance that runs as an existing user against the user's rootless Docker engine, after showing what it does and the exact commands and asking; only the steps still needed run (needs sudo; see qhost help deploy-setup)
- qhost hook-service setup [--listen <host:port>] [--confirm] [--yes]: set up the webhook receiver as its own system user qhost-hook without login and without the groups docker and qhost, or change its address, after showing what it does and the exact commands and asking; only the steps still needed run (needs sudo; see qhost help hook-setup)
- sudo qhost uninstall [--purge] [--confirm] [--yes]: remove both services, the webhook receiver where it is set up, every deploy service instance for a rootless engine, the program, the units and the manpages from the host after showing what it does and asking; --purge deletes the state, the journal of the host changes, the configuration of the receiver, the users and the group qhost too; stack folders and containers are never touched (see qhost help deploy-setup)
- qhost tui, or qhost without a command at a terminal: open the terminal interface with the areas Stacks, Stack detail, Runs, Settings, Containers, Proposals and System; it shows each stack's versions, state, last run and findings, follows the log of a running run, v in Runs opens the log of the service (V chooses its minimum level), and offers update, deploy, pause, pin, resume, start, stop and restart (a stopped stack shows as stopped) and the registry query on the cursor stack or on stacks ticked with space; the stack detail sets and deletes the stack's secrets (value hidden) and restores an older backup; Stacks groups the stacks under a row for each stack folder (with more than one Docker engine, in a section for each engine, the cursor passing from one to the next, and an engine whose deploy service cannot be asked appears as a note), and a adds a folder, F fetches the folder on the cursor, x releases it and e generates, stores and shows its deploy key; Settings shows what applies to the whole host or device on four sub-tabs under the tab line (arrow down leads to them, left and right choose one): General sets the preferences of this device, Credentials sets credentials, Maintenance window changes the maintenance window and Notifications adds, tests and removes notification channels (with several deploy services both show one instance, which H chooses, and never touch another); Containers lists every container of the host by own stack, foreign project and single container with state and a detail, and reads again every 5 seconds while it is shown, and S, X and Z there start, stop and restart the container on the cursor or in the detail after a question, also a foreign or single one, and a on a foreign project takes it over as a stack folder (see qhost help sources); the stack detail lists the containers of its stack; o opens the output of the container on the cursor, or in the stack detail of all containers of the stack, which follows once a second and takes a period with T and a search with /; every change comes after a confirmation; without sudo the interface shows the command sudo qhost ... and runs it after y, and sudo asks for the password; where the deploy service is not set up the areas around Docker say so and G sets it up after a question, without sudo through sudo qhost deploy-service setup; System lists the local users and groups of the host and the permissions of a folder and changes them after a confirmation through sudo qhost host apply, also when the interface runs without sudo, lists the latest changes from the journal and undoes one through sudo qhost host undo, and shows the action log of the host service with the check of its chain (see qhost help host-tui)
- qhost status: show the host service and the deploy service, which is shown as not set up until its setup; for the deploy service its version, start time, last poll and the state of each stack with its engine, every instance for a rootless engine with whether it runs and its stacks with their engine, and the webhook receiver as not set up or running or not running with its address and number of hooks (hook_service, hook_listen, hook_count) (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost key generate|set|show [--dir <path>]: create or store the SSH deploy key of a stack folder and show its public part (show runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; generate and set need sudo)
- qhost repo set <ssh-url> [--dir <path>]: bind a stacks repository to a stack folder, confirm its host key and clone it; a further folder becomes a further source (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost repo fetch: fetch new commits of every stack folder without touching the working copies (run with sudo)
- qhost repo probe <ssh-url>: show the host key and fingerprint a git server presents and whether it is pinned; stores nothing (run with sudo; see qhost help repository)
- qhost source inspect <path> [--url <ssh-url>] [--include <path>]: classify a folder before it is taken over as a stack folder (clone, adopt-clone, local, files or reject), with its permissions, the files and stacks it would bring and container conflicts in all Docker engines of the host; as root it also shows whether the folder is missing, the permission changes source add would make and an effect fingerprint for source add --expect-effect; changes nothing (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost source add <path> [--url <ssh-url>] [--fingerprint <fp>] [--expect-effect <fp>] [--expect-state <s> --expect-commit <c>] [--include <path>] [--yes]: take over a folder as a stack folder (a clone to make, an existing clone, a repository without a remote or a folder of files, which --url can join with a remote) and give the service the access it needs, after a preview and a confirmation (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost source remote <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--expect-state <s> --expect-commit <c>] [--yes]: give a local source a remote repository after comparing the folder with it; secret-like files in what would be pushed stop it (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost source compare <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>]: show how a folder relates to a remote repository (state, branch, commit, differing paths, secret-like files) without changing the folder; the confirmed host key is pinned (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost source list: list the bound stack folders of every deploy service with engine, kind, address, head and the state of the last poll (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost source move <folder> --into <source>: preview moving a running Compose project from a folder outside every source into an existing source: the target folder, the files a commit would take, what is left out, and every block and warning with its reason and fix; changes nothing (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost source remove <path> [--yes]: release a stack folder after a question; no file is deleted and no container is stopped (run with sudo; see qhost help sources)
- qhost stack list: list the stacks of the working copy of every deploy service with engine, image versions, update mode and whether the stack is stopped (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost stack check [stack]: show per stack of the working copy whether deploy, update, rollback with data, backup and the redaction of secrets work with its files, why not and what to change, and the findings; exit code 1 on errors (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; see qhost help stack-capabilities)
- qhost stack deploy <stack>: start a manual run of a stack at the commit the working copy has; the stack is named by its Compose project (see qhost help stack-capabilities), and for a stack with the finding project-renamed this decides to start the new project, next to the old one, which keeps running (run with sudo)
- qhost stack runs [stack] [--engine <engine>]: list the recorded runs, newest first (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost stack log <run> [--engine <engine>]: print the log of a run (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost stack resume <stack>: clear the pause a failed rollback left on a stack, and release a halted automatic repair; it starts no run (run with sudo)
- qhost stack pause|unpause <stack>: keep the maintenance window from running a stack, or lift that; unrelated to the pause of a failed rollback (run with sudo)
- qhost stack pin|unpin <stack>: hold the image versions of a stack, so the maintenance window and qhost stack update leave them alone, or release them (run with sudo)
- qhost stack start|stop|restart <stack> [--yes]: start, stop or restart the containers of a stack after a question; a stop keeps the stack stopped against new commits and the maintenance window until a start, and qhost status and stack list show it (run with sudo; see qhost help containers)
- qhost window show|set: show the maintenance window, in which the service checks the registries and updates the stacks in automatic mode, or change its weekdays, start, duration and time zone; the running service follows at once (show runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; set needs sudo)
- qhost stack update <stack>... [--version <v>]: update the image versions of stacks, each version step a commit of the stack's .env pushed to the branch and a run with backup, check and rollback (run with sudo)
- qhost stack rollback <stack> <run> --restore-data|--keep-data [--yes]: set a stack back to the image versions before an update run of qhost, with the data the update backed up or with the data as it is, as a secured run of its own; it pins the stack afterwards (run with sudo; see qhost help backups)
- qhost stack updates [stack] [--refresh]: show the current and the available image versions per stack and variable, with newer major versions flagged as not automatic; --refresh asks the registries now (without --refresh it runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; --refresh needs sudo)
- qhost stack changelog <stack>: show the release notes between the current and the available version of each variable with an update; qhost stack updates --changelog shows them for all stacks (run with sudo, as it asks GitHub)
- qhost stack backup create|list <stack>, qhost stack backup restore <stack> <backup> [--yes]: back up, list and restore the data of a stack (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, the rest needs sudo; see qhost help backups)
- qhost stack datadir <stack> [--owner UID:GID] [--mode 0750] --yes: create the missing data directories of a stack and deploy it (run with sudo; see qhost help data-directories)
- qhost stack secret set <stack> <KEY>, qhost stack secret list <stack>, qhost stack secret delete <stack> <KEY>: write, list by key and remove the secrets in a stack's secrets.env; the value is read without echo at a terminal or from standard input, never from an argument, and nothing is redeployed (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, set and delete need sudo)
- qhost credential set|list|delete: manage registry tokens and notification credentials (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, set and delete need sudo)
- qhost notify list|add|remove|test: manage the notification channels (webhook, Slack, Telegram, e-mail) and send a test message through one (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, the rest needs sudo; see qhost help notifications)
- qhost proposal submit|list|show|withdraw: ask for a change of the setup (a stack folder, the settings of a stack, the maintenance window or a notification channel) or of the host (a user, a group, folder permissions, or, only while root has switched it on, free commands), without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; nothing on the host changes. qhost proposal approve|reject, sudo qhost host apply --proposal and sudo qhost proposal run: decide on a proposal, only root (see qhost help proposals)
- qhost update [version], qhost update status: update qhost itself to the latest stable release or to a given newer one from its signed GitHub releases and restart the service; status shows the installed version and the latest release and changes nothing (update runs with sudo; without --confirm it shows what it would replace and restart, without asking the release server, and exits with code 2; stack updates are qhost stack update)
- qhost container list [--metrics], qhost container show <name> [--engine <engine>]: show every container of every deploy service, own stacks, foreign Compose projects and single containers, with state and Docker engine; changes nothing (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; see qhost help containers)
- qhost container logs <name>|--stack <stack> [--engine <engine>] [--since T] [--until T] [--tail N] [--follow]: show the output of a container or of a whole stack (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; see qhost help containers)
- qhost service log [--since T] [--level debug|info|warn|error] [--tail N] [--follow] [--source S]: show the log of the qhost service itself, with start and stop, maintenance windows, source calls, warnings and errors; the service keeps a bounded copy of its log with secret values masked (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; the terminal interface shows it with v in Runs)
- qhost audit [--since T] [--until T] [--action A] [--result success|failure] [--limit N]: show the action log of the host service, newest first: one entry per request on its channel and per change of the host, never with an argument or a secret value. qhost audit verify: check the hash chain of the action log day by day; a broken chain ends with exit code 1 (both run without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost host users|user <name>|groups|group <name>|folder <path>: read the local users, groups and folder permissions; changes nothing (run without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; see qhost help host-admin). sudo qhost host file <path>: list a folder or show a file of the host as root
- qhost host apply <action> [args...] --confirm, qhost host apply --proposal <id> --confirm, qhost host undo <journal-id> --confirm, qhost host journal: change the host with a registered action or a proposal of the host that keeps the previous state in a root-only journal, undo a change, and list the latest changes; every change appears in qhost audit; without --confirm apply and undo show the sentence and the commands and exit with code 2 (all need sudo; see qhost help host-admin)
- qhost diagnose [--out <path>]: write a diagnostic package, an archive (tar.gz) with a text file and a JSON file per part, with the versions, the state of the service and the sources, the stacks and their check, the last 20 runs with the logs of the 5 newest and of every failed one, the service log of the last 24 hours and the list of the containers with their engine, but no container logs, env files or compose files; secret values are masked by the service, a part that cannot be collected is in the package with its reason. Without --out it goes to the folder qhost in your home; the file is new, mode 0600 and yours. It holds the host name and paths: check it before you pass it on (runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost)
- qhost container start|stop|restart <name> [--engine <engine>] [--yes]: start, stop or restart one container of the host after a question (run with sudo; see qhost help containers)
- qhost version: print the version
- qhost help <topic>: show a help topic`

const deployServiceTopic = `qhost runs as two services. The host service runs as the system user qhost without access to the container engine. The deploy service rolls out the stacks and runs as the system user qhost-deploy in the group docker, which is practically root, with its own channel in /run/qhost-deploy and its own state in /var/lib/qhost-deploy. The deploy service cannot read the state of the host service or reach its channel.

The installer sets up the host service only. The deploy service exists after a person sets it up:
- qhost deploy-service setup [--confirm] [--yes]: run as root, it shows what it does and the exact commands, asks, and then creates the system group and user qhost-deploy without login, adds the user to docker, writes /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-deploy.conf for the channel directory and the unit qhost-deploy.service, and enables and starts it. The user never joins the group qhost. A repeated setup changes nothing, and one that stopped at a step continues from there. Without --confirm it shows the plan and the commands and changes nothing (exit code 2). --confirm and the question (--yes) are needed as for qhost update.
- The terminal interface offers the same with G, after a question. As root it runs the setup itself; without sudo it shows the command sudo qhost deploy-service setup --confirm --yes (with --rootless <user> where it offers that) and runs it through sudo after y, and sudo asks for the password. A host with only instances for rootless engines counts as set up there: the interface then shows and runs the stacks of every instance, each under its engine, and sends an action to the instance of the stack it was chosen on.
- Until then the commands that need the deploy service end with exit code 3 and the message that it is not set up, and qhost status shows it as not set up.
- qhost update replaces the unit of the deploy service and restarts it only where it is set up. Where instances for rootless engines are set up, the newly installed program then replaces the template qhost-deploy@.service where it differs, reloads systemd and restarts every instance, because the running program does not know the new template (the hidden step qhost deploy-service refresh-instances, which can be run again). A failure there is a warning and does not undo the update. A unit qhost-deploy@<user>.service or a template qhost-deploy@.service in /etc/systemd/system overrides the installed one, and the update warns about it. Without an instance the update does not touch the template. Where the webhook receiver is set up, the newly installed program likewise replaces qhost-hook.service where it differs from its own copy (the unit is not in the release archive), reloads systemd and restarts the receiver (the hidden step qhost hook-service refresh, which can be run again); a failure there is a warning. A host without the receiver gets neither its unit nor a start.
- sudo qhost uninstall [--purge] [--confirm] [--yes]: removes both services from the host. It first shows what it does and the exact commands and asks, like the setup; without --confirm it shows them and changes nothing (exit code 2). It stops and disables qhost and, where set up, qhost-deploy, and deletes the program, both units, the drop-in /etc/systemd/system/qhost-deploy.service.d/10-stack-folders.conf, /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-deploy.conf with the channel directory /run/qhost-deploy, and the manpages. The state in /var/lib/qhost and /var/lib/qhost-deploy, the journal in /var/lib/qhost-host, the users qhost and qhost-deploy and the group qhost stay. With --purge they are deleted too, credentials and backups of the deploy service included, and that cannot be undone. For every instance for a rootless engine that is set up, it also runs systemctl disable --now qhost-deploy@<user>.service and deletes /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-deploy-<user>.conf, the channel directory /run/qhost-deploy-<user>, the drop-in directory of the unit when it is empty and last the template qhost-deploy@.service; with --purge also /var/lib/qhost-deploy-<user> and /etc/qhost-deploy-<user>. Where the webhook receiver is set up, it also runs systemctl disable --now qhost-hook and deletes its unit, /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-hook.conf and the marker folder /run/qhost-hook; with --purge also /etc/qhost-hook with its hooks and the user and group qhost-hook, when they exist. The users, their homes, their rootless engines and containers always stay. The stack folders, their data and the containers are never touched: containers keep running, and a purge that would delete a bound stack folder changes nothing. Only fixed paths are deleted, none from an argument or the environment.

The setup needs the groups docker and qhost: install the container engine and qhost with its installer first.

The rootless Docker engine of a user gets its own deploy service, an instance that runs as that user, so a taken-over stack or instance reaches no more than that user can. It never reaches the system engine or another engine:
- qhost deploy-service setup --rootless <user> [--confirm] [--yes]: run as root, it shows what it does and the exact commands, asks, and then enables lingering for the user when it is off (loginctl enable-linger), so the engine runs without a login, writes the template unit qhost-deploy@.service and /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-deploy-<user>.conf for the channel directory /run/qhost-deploy-<user> (the user and the group qhost, 2750), and enables and starts qhost-deploy@<user>.service. The instance keeps its state in /var/lib/qhost-deploy-<user>, its configuration in /etc/qhost-deploy-<user> and its Docker client settings in the state, uses only the engine at /run/user/<uid>/docker.sock, and a drop-in for it belongs in /etc/systemd/system/qhost-deploy@<user>.service.d/.
- The user must exist, have a rootless engine (its socket or the user unit ~/.config/systemd/user/docker.service) and entries in /etc/subuid and /etc/subgid, and the name must match ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$. A user in the group qhost or docker is refused, because the instance runs with the groups of its user and would reach the channel of the host service or the system engine; change the groups in the area System first. For an administrator the question warns that the damage is no longer limited. The setup never creates a user or changes a group, and a repeated setup changes nothing.
- The channel of an instance admits root and the administrators in the group qhost, as the deploy service does; its own user is not admitted. An instance whose engine does not answer yet keeps running and reports the engine as unreachable.
- An instance counts as set up once its file in /etc/tmpfiles.d exists. qhost status lists every instance as instance.<user>, with whether it runs, and its stack folders and stacks as instance.<user>.source.<id> and instance.<user>.stack.<name>.
- Where the webhook receiver is set up (see qhost help hook-setup), a deploy service set up afterwards, also with --rootless, adds its marker folder /run/qhost-hook/system or /run/qhost-hook/<user> to /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-hook.conf in its plan; otherwise the receiver is not touched.`

const hookServiceTopic = `The webhook receiver is a third service, qhost-hook. It listens on one address, accepts only calls to a secret address of a stack, discards what a call sends and drops an empty marker file for that stack, which the deploy service of the stack reads. It exists after a person sets it up; the installer does not.

- qhost hook-service setup [--listen <host:port>] [--confirm] [--yes]: run as root, it shows what it does and the exact commands, asks, and then creates the system group and user qhost-hook without login, without a home and without any other group, writes /etc/qhost-hook/config.json (owner root, group qhost-hook, mode 0644, no hooks at first) and /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-hook.conf for the marker folders /run/qhost-hook and one folder per deploy service instance (system, or the user of a rootless instance; owner qhost-hook, group of the instance's service user, mode 0770), and writes the unit qhost-hook.service, which it enables and starts. Without --confirm it shows the plan and the commands and changes nothing (exit code 2). --confirm and the question (--yes) are needed as for qhost update. Only the steps still needed run, so a repeated setup changes nothing, and one that stopped at a step continues from there.
- Address: --listen sets it as host:port, with a port above 1024. The first setup without --listen uses 127.0.0.1:8480, which is reachable from this host only; a later setup without --listen keeps the stored address. A different address is written to the configuration, hooks stay as they are, and the receiver restarts.
- HTTPS: the receiver speaks plain HTTP. An address that is not local, such as 0.0.0.0:8480, is named in the question with the advice that a reverse proxy should provide HTTPS; the proxy forwards to the receiver, ideally on the local address.
- A deploy service set up later, for the system engine or with --rootless <user>, adds its marker folder to /etc/tmpfiles.d/qhost-hook.conf in the same plan where the receiver is set up. Where it is not set up, the deploy setup does not touch the receiver.
- What the receiver may do: listen on its address, read its configuration and write marker files into the folders of /run/qhost-hook. It has no capabilities, no home, no write access elsewhere and 64 MB of memory, and the unit is the one the program carries.
- What it may not do: it never reaches the container engine, the host service or the channel of a deploy service, runs no command, reads no body and starts no deploy itself. A caller who knows a secret address can at most trigger the checks that the update rules of the stack allow. The configuration holds only hashes of the secret addresses, and the receiver refuses a configuration that anyone but root can write.`

const credentialsTopic = `The service keeps its own secrets in a private store that only the service user can read. Listing the credentials runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; setting and deleting need sudo, because they change the service.

Each deploy service, the one of the system engine and the instance of each rootless engine, keeps its own deploy keys, credentials and pinned host keys, and none receives those of another. qhost credential, qhost repo probe and qhost key without --dir take --engine system or --engine rootless:<user>; without it they mean the system engine, or, when its deploy service is not set up, the only instance, and with several instances and no deploy service of the system engine they need --engine. qhost key with --dir reaches the engine that has the folder bound; for a folder that is not bound yet it follows the same rule as without --dir, and so does qhost source compare.

Deploy keys:
- Every remote stack folder has its own key, because a git platform accepts a deploy key in one repository only. The first folder uses the key without --dir; every further one needs its own before qhost repo set binds it.
- qhost key generate [--dir <path>]: create an Ed25519 key in the service for the stack folder and print its public part and SHA256 fingerprint; the folder need not be bound yet
- qhost key generate --replace: replace an existing key; without --replace an existing key is kept and the command refuses
- qhost key set [--dir <path>] [--file <path>]: store an existing key, for example from a password manager, instead of generating one; paste it at the prompt (hidden, ends with the END line), pipe it on standard input, or name a key file. It must be an OpenSSH private key without passphrase, Ed25519 or RSA of at least 3072 bits, at most 16 KiB. It prints the public part like generate; --replace replaces an existing key.
- qhost key show [--dir <path>]: print the public part again
- Enter the public part as a deploy key in the git platform of that folder's repository. A folder whose key pushes (a version change or revert, qhost source remote) needs write access to the repository. The private key never leaves the service.

Credentials:
- qhost credential set <name>: store a registry token or notification credential; the value is read without echo at a terminal, or from standard input when piped, and never from an argument
- qhost credential list: show names and modification times, never values
- qhost credential delete <name>: remove a credential
- A name has 1 to 64 characters: lowercase letters, digits, '.', '_' or '-', starting with a letter.
- Registry login for update checks and image pulls: name the credential "registry." plus the registry host in lowercase with ':' written as '-' (Docker Hub: registry.docker.io), with the value <user>:<token>. Without such a credential the registry is asked anonymously. The deploy service takes registry logins only from this store, through its own Docker configuration and credential helper, so a docker login made anywhere else does not apply to its stacks.
- GitHub token for changelogs: name the credential "github.com", with the token alone as value. It is sent only to the GitHub API and raises the rate limit; without it GitHub is asked anonymously.
- A stored value is not shown again and is removed from the service's log output.`

const sourcesTopic = `A host can deploy from several stack folders, each a source with its own clone, engine state, poll and deploy key. Commands on a stack need no source: a stack is named by its Compose project, and the service finds its folder.

Sources:
- qhost repo set <ssh-url> --dir <path> binds a further folder next to those bound earlier; a bound folder is never rebound to another URL, and folders may not lie inside each other (see qhost help repository).
- The id of a source comes from its path and never changes: /opt/stacks becomes opt-stacks, /srv/services/stacks becomes srv-services-stacks.
- Each subfolder of a source with a compose file is a stack. When the root of the source holds a compose file, found as in a subfolder, the source is exactly one stack instead: the whole folder except .qatlas/, named by its Compose project, else by the folder's name. Its subfolders are then part of it, and those with a compose file of their own get the finding ignored-stack-folder.
- Every remote source has its own deploy key: qhost key generate --dir <path> or qhost key set --dir <path> before qhost repo set (see qhost help credentials).
- A source whose fetch or poll fails keeps its state; the other sources go on. qhost status shows each source with its last poll, error and commits, and each stack with its source.
- The maintenance window and qhost stack updates cover the stacks of every source.

Taking over a folder:
- qhost source inspect <path> [--url <ssh-url>] classifies a folder and changes nothing: no file, permission, repository or container is touched, and no value of an env file is shown. --url is the SSH address to clone, or the one the folder must have as origin. It exits 0 for every case, a rejection included; read the case, or the case field of --output json.
- As root, inspect also shows missing (the folder does not exist yet), permissions (the number of paths and a summary of the changes source add would make, only when there are any) and effect, a fingerprint of the case, missing, the listed and left-out files and the permission changes. qhost source add --expect-effect <effect> applies only that: when the effect differs by then, it stops before it changes anything with the code state-changed, and you inspect again. The address, the stacks and the containers are not part of the effect. The choice of unclear files (--include of source inspect and source add, see below) is part of it: take the effect from inspect with the same --include, or add stops with state-changed.
- clone: the folder is missing or empty and would get a clone of the URL; without --url the answer says an address is needed.
- adopt-clone: the folder is a clone with an origin remote, and is taken over as it is; a --url must equal that origin.
- local: the folder is a repository without a remote and runs locally, never pushed. A --url is rejected here, as for a folder with files.
- files: the folder holds files and no repository. The answer lists the files a first commit would take and what it would leave out with the reason. A first commit takes only what the readable stacks of the folder refer to, read from their Compose files without Docker and without showing a value of .env: the Compose files, .env, env_file, label_file, configs file, build context and dockerfile, include, extends file and bind mounts of a file or folder that are read-only; variables in a path are resolved from .env, and one without a value is listed as not resolvable. Always taken: .gitignore, .gitattributes and the stack settings of the folder root. Left out: bind-mount (the data folder of a stack), not-referenced (no stack needs it, such as backup copies, notes and logs; a folder with only such files is one entry), writable-file (a single file a stack mounts writable, which may hold data), secrets-file (the file of a Compose secret), unreadable-stack (the stack cannot be read, so it brings no file; its finding names the file, and for a permission problem asks you to give the group of the deploy service read permission, which qhost never changes itself), too-large, not-regular (not a plain file or folder), unreadable and nested-repo (a folder with a repository of its own). Paths that leave the folder, absolute paths and links are never taken. source add takes it over (see below).
- reject: the folder cannot be taken over. The answer gives the reason and what to do, for example a symbolic link in the path, a folder inside another repository or overlapping another source, a repository with remotes but no origin, an address that differs from the origin, or a folder on a read-only file system.
- Every answer except a rejection shows the owner, group and mode of the folder (or of its nearest existing parent while it is missing), whether the service can write there, and whether permissions would have to be set. For a repository it also says whether the service can write its .git folder.
- For each stack the folder would bring it shows the functions and findings as qhost stack check does, and the containers that already run in the stack's folder: deploying the stack would create duplicate containers and take their ports twice. In the Docker engine of the service these are the containers of other Compose projects. In the rootless Docker engines of the host's users it is every container of a Compose project started from that folder, also under the stack's own project name, because the service does not see that engine. Each conflict names its engine: system, or rootless:<user>. qhost warns and does not block: the question before the takeover and before the approval of a proposal names the consequence, and the folder can still be taken over.
- The rootless engines are read only with sudo, and only through the socket of the user's own engine (/run/user/<uid>/docker.sock, never a link or a socket of another user) with one request for its container list. A user with rootless Docker set up whose engine is not running or does not answer, and every rootless engine when the command runs without sudo, is listed as not checked with the reason, and the rest of the answer still holds. Nothing is started, stopped or changed. In JSON each conflict has an engine field and the answer has engines_not_checked with engine and reason. When the stacks or the container engine cannot be read, the answer says why and the rest still holds.

Source add:
- qhost source add <path> [--url <ssh-url>] [--fingerprint <fp>] [--engine <engine>] [--yes] inspects the folder, shows the case and the changes it would make, and applies them only after you confirm: at a terminal by answering the question, without a terminal with --yes. A rejected folder is refused and nothing changes.
- clone and adopt-clone work like qhost repo set (see qhost help repository): the host key is confirmed at the terminal or with --fingerprint, and the folder needs its deploy key. --yes never confirms a host key.
- local binds the repository without a remote and without a deploy key. The service deploys the commits that people make there and rolls a failing one back with a revert commit in the same folder; nothing is pushed until qhost source remote gives it a remote.
- files shows the list of files and what is left out with the reason (the first 50 of each), and starts a repository only after you confirm: git init with branch main and core.sharedRepository=group, and a first commit of the listed files as qhost. Each path left out becomes a line of a block marked by qhost in the .gitignore of the folder, so a fresh clone ignores it too: a missing .gitignore is created, an existing one keeps its own lines and gets the block at its end (a block of an earlier takeover is extended, never repeated), and the first commit holds it. The preview shows the lines; with nothing to add no file in the folder is written, and the work tree is clean afterwards. A .gitignore that is a symbolic link, cannot be read or is larger than 1 MiB makes the folder rejected. The folder is then bound as local, or as remote with --url (see below). Permissions are set for the folder, the folders of listed files and the listed files. Before it changes anything, source add looks at the folder again and stops if the list or the left-out files differ from what was shown. Files left out are not in the repository: one a stack needs to read stays in the folder and must be readable by the service.
- Unclear files of a folder of files may be data or a secret: a file a stack mounts writable (writable-file, such as acme.json) and the file of a Compose secret (secrets-file). The preview lists them apart from the other left-out files, with the reason and the stack, and they stay out unless you choose them. At a terminal without --yes source add asks for each one (default no). --include <path> (repeatable, a path below the folder) takes a file into the first commit in advance, also with --yes and without a terminal; without a terminal and without --include the file stays out. A path that the service does not classify as unclear itself is refused and nothing changes, and so is a chosen file it cannot take (unreadable, too large, not a plain file). The chosen files are in the list that is confirmed and are marked there; a chosen file with a secret in it is taken like any other, and with --url the check of what a push would publish still applies to it. Only a folder of files has unclear files, and --include for another case is refused. source compare takes --include as well and lists the chosen files as included.
- Permissions are set only when the service cannot write the folder or its .git, as root and without changing an owner: the group of the service account (qhost-deploy, or the primary group of the user of the instance with --engine) for the folder, .git and every folder with versioned files; group write and setgid on those folders; group read and write on versioned files (for files, the listed ones); core.sharedRepository=group. Never changed: the sources of bind mounts and everything below them, unversioned files, symbolic links, files with further hard links and paths outside the folder. An unversioned file the stacks need, such as secrets.env, must therefore already be readable by the service, for example with the group qhost-deploy and mode 0640.
- The folder is then added to the systemd drop-in and the service restarts once, as repo set does.
- --engine <engine> names the Docker engine of the folder: system (the default) or rootless:<user>, for a user whose deploy service instance is set up (qhost deploy-service setup --rootless <user>). The instance of that engine inspects, binds and deploys the folder, and the system engine does not start its stacks. A created folder gets that user as owner, the drop-in is the 10-stack-folders.conf of the unit qhost-deploy@<user>.service, and only that instance restarts. An engine without an instance is refused. The engine never changes silently: when containers of the folder run in another engine, source inspect and source add suggest it with the option.
- Folders and stack names stay unique across all engines. Before it changes anything, source add asks the deploy service of every other engine and refuses a folder that overlaps one of their stack folders and a stack name that one of them has. A service that cannot be asked is named as not checked, and the question then says the check is incomplete: confirm it with the terminal or --yes. repo set --dir asks the same for the folder, and then asks that question too (--yes).
- Every command on a stack or a stack folder reaches the deploy service of the engine that has it: qhost stack deploy, update, rollback, settings, secret, backup, datadir, check, changelog, updates, start, stop, restart, pause, unpause, pin, unpin and resume, qhost stack runs <stack>, qhost container logs --stack, qhost key --dir, qhost repo fetch --dir, qhost source remove and qhost source remote. Only a host with several deploy services is asked first, which runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost. When several engines have a stack of that name, for example after a commit added it to a second folder, the command stops and names them, and --engine system or --engine rootless:<user> chooses one; an engine that is not set up is refused. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning when the stack or folder is found in another engine, and otherwise the error names its engine. qhost stack update takes stacks of one engine per call. qhost stack runs without a stack reads the deploy service of the system engine, else the only instance, or the one --engine names; qhost stack log <run> asks every deploy service and reads the one that knows the run. qhost stack list, qhost source list and qhost status ask every deploy service and show the engine of each entry (system or rootless:<user>) as a column or the field engine; in qhost status the stacks and folders of an instance are keyed below instance.<user>. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning with its engine, not an error.
- qhost source list shows every bound folder of every deploy service with path, id, engine, kind, address (none for a local one), head, last poll, poll error and note.
- qhost source remove <path> [--yes] asks first. It releases only the binding and drops the engine state of the source: no file in the folder is deleted, no container is stopped, and the permissions, the drop-in entry and the deploy key stay, so source add binds the folder again. A source with an active run is refused.

Moving a project into a source:
- qhost source move <folder> --into <source> shows what moving the Compose project in <folder> into the existing source <source> would do, and changes nothing: no file is written, no container is touched, nothing is fetched or committed, and no value of an env file is shown. The folder must lie outside every source and hold one project with its compose file at its root; the project would get the folder <source>/<name of the folder>. Moving renames the folder on the same file system, so the data stays untouched and the way back is renaming it back. The deploy service of the engine that has <source> bound answers, and the command needs sudo like source inspect: the Docker engines of the host are read as there, and the deploy services of the other engines are asked for stack folders and stack names.
- The preview lists the files a commit into the source would take, chosen as for a folder of files (see above), what is left out with the reason, and the lines for the .gitignore of the folder. In JSON, movable is true exactly when blocks is empty; a block or a rejection is a complete answer and exits 0.
- A block means the move cannot be done as it is; each names its code, the reason and what to do. root-stack: the source is one root stack, a second stack does not fit. source-unreadable. target-exists: the target folder exists already. target-overlaps: it overlaps a stack folder of another source or engine. target-name: the folder name starts with a dot. other-filesystem: the folder and the source are on different file systems, so the folder cannot be renamed. engine-differs: containers of the project run in another Docker engine than the source (system or rootless:<user>); moving between engines is not supported. name-taken: a stack of that name exists in any engine. project-name-changes: the Compose project name would change (without name: or COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME it is the name of the folder), so a deploy would create new, empty named volumes; qhost renames nothing. absolute-bind-mount: a bind mount names a path inside the old folder as an absolute path. secrets-in-env: .env holds values of secret-like keys (PASSWORD, SECRET, TOKEN, KEY) and the source has a remote; the keys are named, never the values, and the fix is to move them to secrets.env by hand. folder-rejected, not-a-project, project-is-repository and stack-unreadable: the folder cannot be looked at as a project folder (it overlaps a source, lies in a repository, holds no compose file at its root or several stacks, has a repository of its own, or cannot be read).
- A warning does not block: secrets-in-env for a local source (they are committed; a remote can be given later only after they are moved), fixed-tags (images with a fixed tag cannot be updated by qhost, the stack counts as deploy only; qhost stack check shows the finding), downtime (the containers are recreated from the new folder) and not-checked (an engine or deploy service could not be asked, or the containers could not be read; without sudo no rootless engine is asked).
- qhost never rewrites a compose file or .env to make a project fit: what does not fit is named, and you change it yourself.

Remote for a folder:
- qhost source remote <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--yes] gives a local source a remote repository. qhost source add <path> --url <ssh-url> does it for a folder of files in one step, but only for a repository that already holds commits; take the folder over without --url first for an empty one. The host key is confirmed as for repo set and --yes never confirms it. The folder needs its deploy key first, also while it is a local source (qhost key generate --dir <path> or qhost key set --dir <path>), with write access to the repository (see qhost help credentials).
- qhost source compare <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--include <path>] shows that comparison alone, with the host key confirmed as for repo set and the secret-like files as an answer rather than an error; it changes nothing in the folder and pins the confirmed host key. --expect-state <empty|equal|different> and --expect-commit <commit> of source remote and of source add --url (a folder of files) apply exactly the comparison shown: the commit is empty exactly when the state is empty, and when the fresh comparison differs they stop before any change with the code state-changed.
- Both compare the folder with the branch of the remote and show the state before anything changes. empty (source remote only): the local history is pushed as the branch. equal (the same files): the history of the remote is taken over and no commit is made; the former local history stays under refs/qhost/local-before-remote. different: the paths that differ are listed (the first 50 and their number), and the state of the host becomes one commit on the remote branch and is pushed, or you cancel. The host never overwrites the remote and nothing is forced.
- For a folder of files, the comparison plans the files again with what the remote branch versions: a file of the folder that the remote versions is taken even when no stack refers to it (a README, or a compose file in a folder that is no stack) and is marked as versioned in the remote, so joining removes nothing the remote has, and a folder with the files of the remote stays equal. Everything the remote does not version is chosen as for a folder without --url. A file left out for another reason, such as writable-file, secrets-file, bind-mount, unreadable-stack or unreadable, stays out even when the remote versions it, unless it is a writable-file or secrets-file that you chose with --include. This plan is what you confirm.
- After your confirmation the service acts only on the state and commit that were shown and changes nothing if the remote moved. The source is then remote; for a folder of files the repository starts on the history of the remote.
- Secret check: every commit the push would publish is checked for secrets files, env files with values and private keys. A finding stops the command with secrets-found before anything changes. It names commit, path and kind, never a value, and cannot be overridden: clean the history yourself, then run the command again.

Foreign Compose projects:
- A Compose project that was started outside qhost shows in the Containers area of the TUI as a foreign project, with the folder and the Compose and env files its containers were started with. a on its group, one of its containers or its detail opens the adoption: it only inspects the folder, shows the project, the files from the labels and what works with them, and goes on with the questions of the case (local, files or adopt-clone) as qhost source add does. Nothing changes before the last yes.
- The folder becomes a source of exactly one stack: its compose file must lie directly in the folder. The adoption is refused, with the cause, when the containers carry no absolute working directory, when a Compose or env file lies outside that folder, when the inspection rejects the folder or does not find exactly one stack.
- A warning, which does not block, appears when the project name or the Compose or env files of the running containers differ from those a deploy would pass to Compose: the first deploy then creates new containers instead of taking over the running ones.
- Without the TUI, qhost container list shows the folder and the Compose files, and qhost source add <folder> takes the folder over.

Commits of people in a local folder:
- A person commits as a member of the group qhost-deploy (usermod -aG qhost-deploy <user>, then a new login) or as the service account with sudo -u qhost-deploy git -c safe.directory=<path> .... The folder belongs to another user, so git refuses it until it is marked safe; a group member does that once with git config --global --add safe.directory <path>.
- A person who creates new folders should work with umask 002: a folder made with the usual umask 022 is not group-writable, and the service could not write into it when a revert has to restore files there.

Stack names:
- Compose tells stacks apart only by project name, so a name is deployed from one source alone. When two sources list the same name, the stack already deployed keeps it, else the one of the source bound first; the other gets the finding name-collision and is never deployed until one of them gets another project name.
- qhost status lists such a stack as source.<id>.stack.<name>; the stack that holds the name stays stack.<name>.

Earlier installations:
- A host bound to one repository by an earlier version keeps running: at its first start the service makes the binding its first source, moves the engine state below it and keeps the existing deploy key. Nothing needs to be done.`

const repositoryTopic = `The service clones the stacks repository of the host over SSH with its own deploy key and checks the server with its own known_hosts file; the system's SSH configuration and known hosts are not used. Host key checking is always strict.

Binding:
- qhost key generate (or qhost key set for a key you already have), then enter the public key as a deploy key with write access in the git platform; qhost pushes its version and revert commits with it
- qhost repo set <ssh-url> --dir <path> [--engine <engine>]: the URL is user@host:path or ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path; --dir names the stack folder, is required and has no default
- On first contact the host key and its SHA256 fingerprint are shown. At a terminal you confirm it by answering the question (default no). Without a terminal, pass the expected fingerprint: --fingerprint SHA256:...
- Compare the fingerprint with the one the git platform publishes before you confirm it.
- qhost repo probe <ssh-url> shows the host key, its fingerprint and whether it is pinned without storing anything (run with sudo); pass the fingerprint you verified to --fingerprint of repo set, source add, source remote or source compare.
- The stack folder is any absolute path, for example on another disk; each host chooses its own. It must be empty or already a clone of the same URL. Another folder becomes a further stack folder next to the earlier ones, with its own deploy key from qhost key generate --dir <path> or qhost key set --dir <path>; a bound folder is never rebound to another URL.
- A folder that does not exist is created only after you confirmed its exact path: answer the question at a terminal, or pass --create without one. It gets the mode 0755 and the owner and group of the service account: qhost-deploy:qhost-deploy, or with --engine rootless:<user> that user. Neither the installer, the service nor an update creates a stack folder.
- --engine <engine> names the Docker engine of the stack folder: system (the default) or rootless:<user> for a user whose deploy service instance is set up. That instance clones and deploys the folder (with its own deploy key and known hosts), and the drop-in below is the one of its unit qhost-deploy@<user>.service.d.
- The service runs sandboxed and may write only where the systemd drop-in /etc/systemd/system/qhost-deploy.service.d/10-stack-folders.conf allows it. repo set adds the folder there as ReadWritePaths=-<path> (entries are only ever added), reloads systemd and restarts the service once. A bound folder without that entry, as after an update from an older release, is added when you next run qhost as root.
- The terminal interface offers the same steps in the Stacks area, also without sudo: there each step that needs root runs as sudo qhost after a question that shows the command.
- qhost repo set is the short form of qhost source add for cloning into an empty or missing folder and for taking over an existing clone; source add also binds a repository without a remote (see qhost help sources).

Afterwards:
- qhost repo fetch: fetch new commits into the remote-tracking branch and report whether the working copy is behind; the working copy itself is not changed
- qhost stack list: list each stack with its image version variables, their values, its update mode (none without settings) and its update trigger with the interval for interval
- A host key that differs from the pinned one aborts every command. The pinned key is never replaced by a command; verify the change with the git platform's operator first.`

const stackSettingsTopic = `The settings of a stack say how qhost updates, backs up and checks it. They live in the stacks repository as .qatlas/qhost/<stack>.yaml in its root, one file per stack, named by the stack's Compose project name. A stack without settings is deployed but never updated.

The settings are shown and changed only in the terminal interface or with qhost stack settings <stack>, qhost stack settings set <stack> and qhost stack settings remove <stack>; nobody edits the file by hand. The stack detail of the terminal interface has the form Update rules (key e): mode, ceiling, major jump, the observation time in minutes (0 off), the trigger (maintenance window, polling or webhook only) with the interval in seconds for polling, and, per image version variable, a tag filter and a changelog repository. The form Backup (key b) sets the data directories, the volumes ticked from the named volumes of the stack's compose files (a saved volume the files no longer have stays and is marked), the command as a list of arguments, and the retention (keep, maximum age in days). The form Compose files (key C) sets the compose files as a list that can be reordered, at most 16; an empty list removes the entry and leaves the files to the stack's own detection. The form Check (key k) sets the container check on or off and the HTTP check: with it on, the address, the expected status (100 to 599, 200 by default) and the timeout (1s to 10m, 10s by default); with it off the three fields are ignored and the section holds no probe. Each form saves its own section only: saving asks first, commits, and in a remote stack folder pushes, and leaves the other sections as they are. Remove deletes the settings file by a commit, with all its sections, and leaves the stack deployed but never updated; the stack detail offers it as the action Remove settings (key E) after a question marked as far-reaching, and cancelling changes nothing. A stack without settings is reported and nothing is committed. qhost checks them against the stack, writes the file, commits it and pushes the commit. A settings commit does not touch the stack folder and starts no run; the settings act on the next update or deploy. The file is read as data only: unknown fields, a missing version, or a path that is absolute or leaves the stack folder are rejected.

Fields (all optional except version):
- version: 1
- compose.files: the compose files of the stack as a list of relative paths inside the stack folder, in merge order like COMPOSE_FILE; it takes precedence over COMPOSE_FILE in .env and over the default names
- update.mode: manual (default) or automatic
- update.trigger: window (default, the maintenance window checks the stack), interval (qhost queries the registries of the stack regularly, also outside the window) or webhook (an external call starts the check); the maintenance window handles only stacks with the trigger window. With interval or webhook, a check that finds an update updates a stack in automatic mode at once, outside the window and kept back by the same marks as in it, and reports a stack in manual mode once per newly available version. A registry that limits requests or cannot be reached doubles the wait of its stacks after each such check, up to 30 minutes (a longer interval stays as it is), until a check of it succeeds
- update.interval: seconds between two registry queries, from 30 to 86400 (default 60); allowed only with update.trigger: interval and refused otherwise
- update.ceiling: patch, minor (default) or major; the largest version step taken; versions are MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH (SemVer or CalVer), and with CalVer the first part, for example the year, counts as major
- update.major: stepwise (default, one major version at a time) or direct
- update.observe: observation time in minutes after an update by qhost (default 60, 0 off, at most 1440); a failure of the stack in it rolls the update back (see qhost help containers); a commit that did not come from qhost starts none; qhost status shows a running one as observe with its end and the update commit
- update.tags.<VARIABLE>: regular expression a candidate tag must match; VARIABLE is the image version variable in .env
- update.changelog.<VARIABLE>: GitHub repository (owner/name or a github.com address) whose releases are shown as changelog; it takes precedence over the image label org.opencontainers.image.source
- backup.paths: data directories below data/ in the stack folder, for example data/db; other paths are refused when a backup runs
- backup.volumes: volume keys of the Compose file (the top-level volumes: names), not Docker volume names
- backup.command: a command as a list of arguments, never a shell line (see qhost help backups)
- backup.retention.keep: number of newest backups to keep (default 7 when no retention is set)
- backup.retention.max_age_days: age limit in days; a backup is kept only while it satisfies every limit that is set
- check.containers: require all containers to be running (default true)
- check.http.url: http or https URL to probe
- check.http.status: expected status code (default 200)
- check.http.timeout: probe timeout from 1s to 10m (default 10s)
- backup needs at least one of paths, volumes, command

Example:
version: 1
update:
  mode: automatic
  ceiling: minor
  tags:
    DEMO_APP_VERSION: '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$'
backup:
  paths: [data/db]
  retention: {keep: 5}
check:
  http: {url: 'http://localhost:8080/health', status: 200, timeout: 5s}

Image versions stand as variables in the stack's .env, for example image: registry.example.test/demo/app:${DEMO_APP_VERSION}. A fixed or floating tag such as latest leaves the image deploy only; qhost stack check names it (see qhost help stack-capabilities).`

const backupsTopic = `A backup saves the data of one stack while the stack is stopped, so the copy is consistent. What is saved is listed in the backup section of the stack settings (see qhost help stack-settings). Backups are kept in the service's state directory under backups/<stack>/<backup>, readable by the service user only.

Commands (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, the rest needs sudo; the service must run):
- qhost stack backup create <stack>: stop the stack if it runs, save every listed data directory and volume, run the backup command, start the stack again, then remove backups beyond the retention limits
- qhost stack backup list <stack>: the backups of a stack, newest first, with time, deployed commit, size and number of parts
- qhost stack backup restore <stack> <backup> [--yes]: replace the data with the content of one backup; the stack is stopped and started again if it ran
- qhost stack rollback <stack> <run> --restore-data|--keep-data [--yes]: set the stack back to the image versions before an update run and, with --restore-data, restore the backup that update made (see Return below)

How it works:
- A data directory or volume is packed into a tar archive with owners and permissions by a short-lived helper container without network. The helper image is set with qhost deploy-service run --backup-image; it needs sh, find, rm and a tar that knows --numeric-owner.
- backup.command runs on the host, not in a container, as a program in the search path or an absolute path, in the stack folder and without a shell; its standard output is saved as command.out in the backup. A relative path such as ./dump.sh is refused.
- The backup records the stack, the time, the commit deployed at that time, the image versions and the user namespace of the engine it was made in (the ID mapping, read in the helper container).
- A backup belongs to the engine it was made in, because the owners in its archives are relative to the user namespace. A deploy service for a rootless engine keeps its own backups in its own state directory and pulls the helper image into its own engine on first use; set --backup-image for it in a drop-in if the image is not to come from the registry.
- A failed backup is removed and a stack that ran is started again.
- A backup and a deployment of the same stack never run at the same time.
- Every deployment run of a stack whose settings have a backup section makes a backup after it stopped the stack; when the run is rolled back, that backup is restored. These backups count for retention like any other.

Retention:
- backup.retention.keep and backup.retention.max_age_days apply after each successful backup; a backup is kept only while it satisfies every limit that is set. The newest backup is never removed.

Restore:
- The backup id comes from qhost stack backup list; the newest is never chosen for you.
- At a terminal the command asks first (default no). Without a terminal, and with --agent, it refuses unless --yes is given.
- Every archive is read through before the first target is emptied. Each target is then emptied and unpacked inside the helper container, on the mounted path only.
- A backup that holds a data directory or volume the settings no longer list is refused as a whole.
- A restore in an engine with another user namespace is refused as a whole before the stack is stopped or anything is emptied, also when it is the rollback of a deployment run; the run then stops with a failed rollback and the stack stays stopped. There is no option to override it. A namespace that cannot be read counts as another one. A backup from before the namespace was recorded counts as made by the system engine: it restores there and is refused in a rootless engine.
- The command output is not restored; the settings have no restore command.
- A restore that fails after the first target was emptied leaves the stack stopped, because it would run on incomplete data.
- A volume must exist before it is backed up or restored; deploy the stack first.

Return:
- qhost stack rollback takes the id of a successful update run by qhost from qhost stack runs; an older run is fine. The image version variables that update changed go back to their value before it; other variables stay.
- Exactly one of --restore-data and --keep-data is required. --restore-data puts back the backup that update made, so everything written to the data since is lost; it is refused before anything changes when that backup is gone or the stack has no backup rules to save the current data first. --keep-data leaves the data, but the older version may not understand data a newer one migrated.
- At a terminal the command shows the versions and asks first (default no). Without a terminal, and with --agent, it refuses unless --yes is given.
- The return is a secured run of its own: it stops the stack, backs up the current data, commits the old versions to .env and pushes, restores the backup with --restore-data, then pulls, starts and checks. A failing run is rolled back with the backup of the current data and a revert of the commit, and the stack is not pinned.
- After a success the stack is pinned, so the maintenance window, polling and webhooks do not move it on and no observation time begins; qhost stack unpin releases it. A pinned stack can be returned again.`

const stackCapabilitiesTopic = `qhost stack check reads the working copy of the stacks repository and reports for each stack which functions of qhost work with its files, which do not, why, and what a person would change. qhost never changes these files itself. The TUI shows the same report at the end of the stack detail.

Functions, in this order:
- deploy: works when qhost can read the stack: its compose files exist and are safe, its settings are valid, secrets.env, if there is one, is a regular file of at most 1 MiB, and every image version variable has a value. An .env with lines qhost cannot parse still deploys, unless such a line is a COMPOSE_ setting, because Compose reads it itself; the stack is then deploy-only.
- update, with the mode automatic, manual or none: works when the stack deploys, qhost can read .env, at least one image takes its version from a variable in .env whose value has a MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH core (SemVer or CalVer, x.y.z), and the stack settings exist. Without settings the mode is none and updates are not switched on. An image with a fixed tag, a digest, no tag or a floating tag such as latest is deploy only and is left alone; a version that only comes from a default in the compose file, or a variable that is the tag of two different images, stops the update of the whole stack.
- rollback: the rollback with data. A failed run of a stack that was deployed before goes back to the previous commit; it restores the data only when the stack settings have a backup section, from the backup the run made before the change.
- backup: works when the stack settings have a backup section, every backup path lies below data/, and the stack name has lowercase letters, digits, - and _ only and at most 63 characters (see qhost help backups).
- redact: the values of secrets.env and of the .env keys whose name contains PASSWORD, SECRET, TOKEN or KEY are removed from the service log, the run logs and the notifications. It does not work for a secrets.env that is a link or too large, and finds nothing when the stack has neither.

Findings (rule ID, severity):
- missing-compose-file (error): a file named in compose.files or COMPOSE_FILE does not exist
- invalid-compose-file (error): a compose.files or COMPOSE_FILE entry is unsafe or not a regular file
- invalid-rules-file (error): the stack settings are invalid
- unknown-rules-variable (error): the stack settings name an image variable the stack does not have
- unreadable-stack (error, or warning for an .env qhost cannot parse): a file of the stack cannot be read
- unreadable-secrets-env (error): secrets.env is a link or too large
- unset-image-variable (error): an image version variable has no value, so the image has an empty tag
- tracked-secrets-env (error with a remote, else warning): git tracks secrets.env, so its secrets reach the remote
- duplicate-key (warning): a key stands in both .env and secrets.env; keep it in one file
- secrets-env-not-ignored (warning): .gitignore does not cover secrets.env
- tracked-data (warning): git tracks files below data/
- old-rules-file (warning): the stack folder holds a qhost.yaml, which is no longer read; set the stack settings instead and delete it
- ignored-stack-folder (warning): a subfolder holds a compose file but is part of the one stack whose compose file is in the root of the stack folder

A finding is an error only when the stack cannot be deployed or a secret would reach a remote; everything else is a warning or only part of the functions. The exit code is 1 when there is at least one error.

Layout of a stack folder:
- one or more compose files: the list compose.files in the stack settings, else COMPOSE_FILE in .env (entries separated by ':', or by COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR), else the first of compose.yaml, compose.yml, docker-compose.yaml, docker-compose.yml
- .env (optional): non-secret values such as image versions, domains, ports, COMPOSE_FILE and COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME
- secrets.env (optional): the secrets, set with qhost stack secret set or in the stack detail of the TUI; kept out of git with .gitignore
- data/ (optional): the data directories of the host, kept out of git

A stack is named by its Compose project name, which is not necessarily its folder: COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME in .env, else the top-level name: of the compose files, else the folder name. This name is used by every command. When the project name of a deployed stack changes, both names get the finding project-renamed and nothing is deployed automatically: the old project keeps running and is not stopped. qhost stack deploy <new name> is the decision to start the new project next to the old one, with new volumes prefixed by the new name; the old project must be stopped by a person.

Secret values are never shown or logged; the check reads only the key names of secrets.env. Compose runs with --env-file for each of .env and secrets.env that exists.`

const dataDirectoriesTopic = `A data directory is a bind mount source inside the stack folder that git does not track, for example ./data/db. If it is missing when a container starts, Docker creates it owned by root, and an image that runs as another user then cannot write to it.

The finding data-dir-missing:
- Before a run touches the stack, the service reads the bind mount sources inside the stack folder from the rendered Compose configuration and finds the user each image runs as. A missing, unversioned directory of a service that does not run as root stops the run with this finding, which lists the paths relative to the stack folder and the owner (UID:GID) the image user suggests, when it is known.
- The finding clears and the same commit runs by itself as soon as all listed directories exist. A manual run (qhost stack deploy) checks again. Nothing is created without you.
- Directories of services that run as root, named volumes, files and tracked paths are not part of the finding.

Resolving it (run with sudo):
- In the terminal interface, open the stack detail, check the owner and mode, and confirm the creation. Without sudo the question shows the command sudo qhost stack datadir with that owner and mode and runs it through sudo after y; sudo asks for the password.
- qhost stack datadir <stack> [--owner UID:GID] [--mode 0750] --yes creates the listed directories and then starts a run of the stack. Without --yes it asks at a terminal and otherwise only prints what it would create. The owner comes from --owner or from the finding; the mode is 0750 unless given.
- Existing directories are never changed, missing parents are created like the listed directories, and nothing outside the stack folder is touched. A symlink on the way refuses the creation.
- If you create the directories yourself, set the owner the container user needs; the service takes them as they are.`

const notificationsTopic = `A notification channel receives the events it selected. Channels belong to a deploy service, not to a stack: the one of the system engine and the instance of each rootless engine keep their own, and the commands take --engine as qhost credential does (see qhost help credentials). Commands (list runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost, the rest needs sudo; the service must run):
- qhost notify list: the channels with type, events and settings, never their secrets
- qhost notify add <name> --type webhook|slack|telegram|smtp [--events <list>] [settings]: add a channel
- qhost notify remove <name>: remove a channel and its stored secrets
- qhost notify test <name>: send one test message once, without retries; a failure carries the error class of the delivery (auth, permission, not-found, rate-limited, unreachable, timeout, tls, config-invalid, invalid-request or runtime) in its message and exit code

Events (--events takes a comma list; without it a new channel receives all; a stored channel keeps the events it selected):
- succeeded: a run deployed a commit
- failed: a run failed and no rollback was made
- rolled-back: a run failed and the stack was rolled back to the previous state; with cause observation, the stack failed in the observation time after an update and qhost rolled that update back
- rollback-failed: a run failed and the rollback failed too; the stack is stopped and paused
- container-failed: the failure monitor found a container of an own stack failed (see qhost help containers); one message per failure with stack, kind, container and the time since which it failed
- recovered: that failure ended; names the same container and how long it lasted, and whether qhost restarted it
- recovery-failed: three repair attempts did not end the failure (see qhost help containers); sent once, names stack, kind, container, the time since which it failed and the number of attempts; with cause backup-missing, the stack failed in the observation time but the backup of the update is missing, so the update was not rolled back and nothing was changed
- update-available: a newer image version was found for a stack in manual mode, once per version

Channel types and settings:
- webhook: posts JSON (event, stack, run, commit, error, items; for container-failed, recovered and recovery-failed also kind, container and since; recovered and recovery-failed also at; recovered also restarted, recovery-failed also attempts; cause is observation for rolled-back and rollback-failed after a failure in the observation time, backup-missing with error for recovery-failed) to an https URL (plain http only to the local machine), signed with a key. The headers X-Qhost-Event, X-Qhost-Timestamp and X-Qhost-Signature carry the event, the Unix time and the signature over the timestamp and the body.
- slack: posts text to an incoming-webhook URL.
- telegram: sends text to a chat; needs --chat-id, and --base-url for a Bot API server of your own.
- smtp: sends an e-mail; needs --host, --from and --to (comma list or repeated); --port (default 587 with starttls, 465 with tls), --security starttls (default) or tls, and --user for a login. The connection is always encrypted.

Secrets: the webhook URL and its signing key, the Slack URL, the Telegram bot token and the SMTP password. They are never an argument or a flag value. At a terminal they are asked for without echo; piped on standard input they are lines in that order (webhook: URL, then key; smtp: the password only with --user). The service stores them as credentials, shows them nowhere and removes them from its log output.

Delivery runs in the background after the event; a failing channel is retried a few times and never delays or fails a run. A delivery that still fails is noted in the log of its run (qhost stack log <run>) and in the service log.`

const containersTopic = `qhost container list and qhost container show read the containers of the whole host from the container engine and change nothing. qhost container start, stop and restart act on one container. Listing, showing and logs run without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost; start, stop and restart need sudo. The service must run.

Commands:
- qhost container list [--metrics]: every container of every deploy service, grouped, each with its engine; add --output json for the groups with their engine
- qhost container show <name> [--engine <engine>]: one container by name or ID, with its engine, group, state, ports, mounts, networks, labels and the names of its environment variables
- qhost container logs <name> [--engine <engine>]: the output (stdout and stderr) of one container by name or ID; with --stack <stack> the merged output of all containers of an own stack, each line with its container. Not the protocol of a run: that is qhost stack log <run>
- qhost container start|stop|restart <name> [--engine <engine>] [--yes]: act on one container by name or ID, also a foreign or single one

Engines: a host has the system engine and, per user, at most one rootless engine, and every engine has its own deploy service. list asks all of them and shows the engine of each group (system or rootless:<user>), sorted by kind, name and engine. A container is addressed by engine and name, because a name is unique only within an engine: start, stop, restart, show and logs ask every deploy service, and when several engines have a container of that name the command stops and names them; --engine chooses one. An action never falls to another engine: when a deploy service cannot be asked, an action stops and names it, show and logs warn. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning in list, and the others are still shown. Engines that have no deploy service are shown by list only to root (sudo), read-only and with a note, and no command acts on their containers; without root list says that such engines are shown only with sudo, because whether there are any cannot be told without it. No deploy service reaches the engine of another.

Kinds of a group:
- own: a Compose project that is a stack of the bound stacks repository and was started from that stack's folder
- foreign: any other Compose project; it shows its working directory and compose files
- single: a container that belongs to no Compose project
- conflict: a foreign project that has the name of a stack of this host but was started from another folder; it is not the stack and is marked in the list

Logs: --since and --until take a duration back from now (90s, 15m, 2h) or an RFC 3339 time; --tail shows the last n lines (default 100, at most 5000, 0 for all); --follow asks about once a second for new lines until Ctrl-C (exit code 0). Secret values of the stacks are masked and control characters replaced; --output json prints one object per line. The service answers in pages and the command continues at the time of the last line, so no line is repeated or missed; lines with the same time are never split between pages.

State of a group is the worst state of its containers, in this order: unhealthy, restarting, stopped, starting, ok. The uptime of a group is that of its youngest running container; restarts are summed.

Actions: the command names the container and its kind and asks first (default no); --yes answers the question, and without a terminal --yes is required. In the terminal interface (qhost tui), S, X and Z in the area Containers do the same for the container on the cursor or in the open detail, after a question that names the container and its kind (default cancel) and act by the ID the container has at that moment; they do nothing on a group header. The result shows the state afterwards, and a container that is not ready counts as failed. A stop signals the container and kills it after ten seconds; stopping a stopped or starting a running container changes nothing. After a start or restart the service waits up to a minute until the container runs and, if it has a health check, is healthy; if it is not ready by then the command shows the state and fails. A container of a stack of this host is refused while a run of that stack is active.

Stacks: qhost stack start|stop|restart <stack> [--yes] act on all containers of an own stack of the working copy, ask first like the container actions and are refused while a run of the stack is active. A stop stops the containers without removing them and sets the mark "stopped": new commits of the stacks repository start no run (the stack shows the finding stopped and its pending commit), the maintenance window skips it, and qhost status and qhost stack list show "stopped since <time>". Only qhost stack start clears the mark; it starts the existing containers, never creates any (a stack without containers fails; deploy it with qhost stack deploy), clears the mark once Compose has started them and then runs the stack's check. qhost stack restart restarts the containers and runs the check; on a stack stopped through qhost it is refused with the hint to use start. Stopping a single container with qhost container stop sets no mark. A stopped stack is refused by every command that would run it (qhost stack deploy, qhost stack update, and likewise the maintenance window, which skips it); start it first.

Metrics: --metrics adds CPU (100 per fully used CPU) and memory of the running containers. Reading them takes about two seconds per running container, in parallel and within a time limit, so the command may wait up to two minutes. If a reading fails or the time runs out, the list says that the metrics are incomplete and the container shows none.

Container engine: the service uses one engine socket for every Docker access (container list and actions, logs, monitoring, Compose, checks, backups and restores), by default /var/run/docker.sock; qhost deploy-service run --docker-socket <path> names another one. A DOCKER_HOST in the service's own environment has no effect. If the engine cannot be reached, the service user may not use it, or it does not answer in time, the command says so and shows nothing.

Failure monitor: every 30 seconds the service checks the containers of own stacks and reports a failure once, as the finding container-failed in qhost status and as the notification event container-failed; when the failure ends it sends recovered. The kinds:
- exited: a container exited with a non-zero code or is dead (an exit with code 0, such as an init job, is fine)
- unhealthy: its health check has been failing for more than 60 seconds
- restart-loop: its restart count rose by 3 or more within 5 minutes
- missing: a service that was running at the last good reading is gone
Nothing is checked while a run or an action of the stack is active, nor for stopped (qhost stack stop), paused (either pause) and foreign stacks. A container stopped with qhost container stop is no failure.
Repair: the monitor also repairs a failure by restarts, each under the run slot of the stack. Attempt 1 comes one minute after the failure began and restarts the failed containers; attempts 2 and 3 come 5 and 15 minutes after the previous one and restart the stack. In the observation time after an update by qhost (qhost help stack-settings, update.observe), a failure gets only attempt 1; if it lasts until attempt 2 would be due, qhost rolls that update back instead of attempts 2 and 3: it stops the stack, restores the backup of the update, reverts the update commit and starts and checks the stack again. Data written since the update is lost. A failed rollback stops and pauses the stack; a missing backup halts the repair without any change. If the failure persists after the third, the repair is halted: qhost status shows "repair" as halted, recovery-failed is sent once and no further attempt is made until qhost stack resume releases it. The attempts count until the stack has run an hour without a failure. Stopped (qhost stack stop), paused (either pause) and foreign stacks are not repaired; a stop or pause ends a repair under way, its count stays.

Environment: only the names of the variables are shown, never their values.`

const proposalsTopic = `A proposal asks for a change of the setup or of the host. An administrator in the group qhost, who needs no sudo to read, submits it; the service of its kind checks and stores it. The host service keeps the kinds host-change and command, a deploy service every other kind, and neither sees the proposals of the other; the id of a proposal of the host starts with h-, every other with p-. A host has one deploy service for the system engine and one for each rootless engine. A proposal belongs to the engine of the deploy service that stored it, carries no engine itself, and is applied only in that engine. Submitting changes nothing on the host: the service does not touch the proposed folder or account, uses no network and runs no git. Only a person decides: root at a terminal approves or rejects it.

Commands submit, list, show and withdraw run without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost:
- qhost proposal submit --kind source-add|stack-settings|window|notify-channel|host-change|command [--engine <id>] [--file <path>|-]: read the content as JSON from the file or from standard input, send it to the service of its kind and print the id of the new proposal. --engine names the deploy service that stores it, system or rootless:<user>; without it the system engine, else the only instance, and with several instances and no system engine the command needs --engine. The kinds host-change and command take no --engine. The file is read as the calling user, at most a little over 64 KiB.
- qhost proposal list: list the proposals of the host service and of every deploy service, newest first, with id, kind, service (host or deploy), engine, state, submitter and times. A service that is not running or not set up is named on standard error and the proposals of the others are listed; the command fails only when none answers.
- qhost proposal show <id> [--engine <id>]: show one proposal with its fingerprint, submitter and content; for a proposal of the host also the sentence, the commands and the proposed SSH keys with type, fingerprint and comment
- qhost proposal withdraw <id> [--engine <id>]: close an open proposal as withdrawn; only its submitter or root may do that
show, withdraw, approve and reject look the p- id up in every deploy service. An unknown id names the engines asked; an id that two engines hold stops the command and names both, and --engine chooses one. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning, or the error when the id is in none of the others, and the command never reaches another engine instead.

Commands approve and reject need sudo and a person at a terminal; they refuse --agent, and there is no --yes. The terminal interface decides the proposals of the deploy service without sudo too: it shows the command with the fingerprint of the proposal as it was shown and runs it through sudo after y, and sudo asks for the password:
- sudo qhost proposal approve <id> [--engine <id>] [--url <ssh-url>] [--fingerprint <fingerprint> --confirm]: show the engine of the proposal, then the proposal and, below it, its effect. For source-add it is worked out with the same check as qhost source add (case, permissions, conflicts, file list). You confirm the host key of the server yourself, because a proposal never does, add the deploy key if the folder has none, and answer a last question. Right before anything changes the proposal is read again and the effect is worked out again; if either differs from what you saw, nothing is applied and the new effect is shown. The change is then made the way qhost source add makes it, in the engine of the proposal: in a rootless engine the folder is taken over into its instance as qhost source add --engine does. When containers of the folder run in another engine, the approval warns that deploying would create duplicate containers and take their ports twice, and does not block; a change of engine means rejecting the proposal and submitting it again, or taking the folder over by hand. --url replaces the proposed address. For a folder of files the unclear files are listed apart; the question asks for each (default no), or --include <path> (repeatable) chooses a file in advance, also with --fingerprint. The choice belongs to the effect the approval is bound to, so take --expect-effect from qhost source inspect with the same --include. --fingerprint, the fingerprint of the proposal as you reviewed it with qhost proposal show, with --confirm approves without a question and refuses a proposal with another fingerprint; without --confirm the command stops after showing the effect (exit code 2). The secret parts of a notify-channel proposal then come on standard input as JSON, and a process without a terminal on standard input is accepted when it has a controlling terminal of its own. The other kinds are described below.
- sudo qhost proposal reject <id> [--engine <id>] [--reason <text>]: close an open proposal as rejected; the reason is kept at most 300 characters long
- sudo qhost host apply --proposal <id> [--fingerprint <fingerprint>] --confirm: apply a proposal of the host, described with the kind host-change below

Kind source-add proposes a folder as a stack folder (see qhost help sources). Its content is a JSON object with these fields and no others:
- path: the absolute, clean path of the folder (required)
- url: the SSH address of its repository, for example ssh://git.example.test/stacks.git (optional)
Example: {"path": "/srv/stacks", "url": "ssh://git.example.test/stacks.git"}

Kind stack-settings proposes the settings of a stack (see qhost stack settings --help). Its content is a JSON object with these fields and no others:
- stack: the name of a stack the service deploys; an unknown stack is refused when submitting and when approving
- settings: the settings object of qhost stack settings, with the members version (1, required), compose, update, backup and check; it is checked with the same rules as qhost stack settings set
Example: {"stack": "web", "settings": {"version": 1, "update": {"mode": "manual"}, "backup": {"paths": ["data"]}}}
The schema has no secret member, so a secret is not proposable: a proposal with a field the schema does not name is refused, never trimmed. Approving shows the difference to the settings that apply now, every value in full and broken into lines, a backup command marked because it runs on the host later; the settings are then written the way qhost stack settings set writes them (a commit, pushed when the folder has a remote).
Kind window proposes the maintenance window of the host (see qhost window set --help). Its content is a JSON object with these fields and no others, at least one of them; a part left out keeps its value:
- days: weekday names such as ["mon", "wed"], or ["daily"]
- start: the start time HH:MM on the 24-hour clock
- duration: whole minutes such as 2h or 1h30m, from 1m to 12h
- zone: local or an IANA time zone name
Example: {"days": ["mon", "thu"], "start": "02:30", "duration": "90m", "zone": "UTC"}
The values are checked with the same rules as qhost window set, so a wrong weekday, time, duration or zone is refused when submitting, and a field the schema does not name, a secret included, is refused, never trimmed. Approving shows the difference to the window that applies now, every value in full; the window is then set the way qhost window set sets it.
Kind notify-channel proposes a notification channel (see qhost help notifications). Its content is a JSON object with these fields and no others: the fields of qhost notify add without its secret parts.
- name: 1 to 32 characters, lowercase letters, digits or '-', starting with a letter (required); a name that is taken is refused when submitting and when approving
- type: webhook, slack, telegram or smtp (required)
- events: the events the channel receives; none means all
- smtp only: host, port, security (starttls or tls), user, from and to (a list of recipients)
- telegram only: chat_id and optionally base_url
The secret parts are the webhook or Slack address with its token, the signing key of a webhook, the bot token and the SMTP password. The schema has no member for them: a proposal that names secret or key, or any other field the schema does not name, is refused, never trimmed. The fields are checked with the same rules as qhost notify add, without secrets and without any network access, so a wrong type, event or setting of another type is refused when submitting.
Example: {"name": "ops-alerts", "type": "telegram", "events": ["failed", "rolled-back"], "chat_id": "-1001234567890"}
Approving shows the type, the name, the events and every field in full, broken into lines. You then enter the secret parts as qhost notify add asks for them, hidden at the terminal; they are never kept on the proposal or in its result. Right before the channel is added, the proposal is read again and the channels are checked again; if the proposal changed or a channel of that name exists now, nothing is added. The channel is then added the way qhost notify add adds it.
Kind host-change proposes one change of the host with an action of qhost help host-admin. Its content is a JSON object with these fields and no others:
- action: the id of the action, for example user.create, group.set-members or folder.set-permissions (required)
- args: the arguments of the action as a list, exactly as qhost host apply takes them; they are checked against the schema of the action and never evaluated by a shell
- keys: public SSH keys in authorized_keys format, one for user.add-key and any number up to 16 for user.create, no other action takes any
Example: {"action": "user.create", "args": ["anna", "group=developers"], "keys": ["ssh-ed25519 AAAA... anna@laptop"]}
Every action of the list may be proposed except user.set-password, user.set-admin and the file actions. Also refused when submitting: user.create with the switch admin, any change of the members of the groups sudo and qhost with group.set-members, because that is administrator access, a password anywhere in the content, an SSH key that is not a valid public key, and a field the schema does not name. user.create is proposed without its initial password: the person who approves enters it. The protection rules of qhost help host-admin and whether the named accounts, groups and paths exist are checked when the proposal is applied, not when it is submitted.
sudo qhost host apply --proposal <id> reads the proposal from the host service, checks it again with the same rules, shows the sentence and the commands on standard error as a change by hand shows them, reads the proposal once more right before the change and applies nothing if it differs from what was shown, takes the approval at the host service, and makes the change the way qhost host apply makes it, with journal, protection rules, undo and the entry in qhost audit; the result is kept on the proposal. The report is the one of qhost host apply with the field proposal_id. The proposed SSH keys come from the proposal. Without --fingerprint you are asked y or n at the terminal; with --fingerprint, the fingerprint of the proposal as you reviewed it with qhost proposal show, there is no question. A user.create proposal reads the initial password from standard input as {"password": "..."} and therefore needs --fingerprint. --agent and a process without a terminal are refused, and there is no --yes.
A proposal holds configuration only, never a secret or a host key fingerprint; a field the kind does not name, and an unknown kind, are refused. The kind command is the one exception, described next.

Kind command proposes shell commands for a change that no registered action covers. It is for the case where nothing else fits, it is off until root turns it on, and it carries risks that every other kind does not:
- Nothing checks the commands, nothing works out what they would change, and nothing can undo them. Whoever runs them runs arbitrary text of the submitter as root.
- A harmless-looking reason proves nothing. An agent that reads foreign content, such as a web page, an issue or a log, can be led to submit a harmful command with a harmless reason. Read every command, not only the reason.
- qhost never runs the commands by itself. A person runs them with sudo qhost proposal run, and only while the switch is on.
The switch is kept by the host service and is off by default:
- sudo qhost proposal commands enable|disable: turn it on or off. Off, the service refuses a new proposal of this kind with the code commands-disabled, refuses to approve one and qhost proposal run refuses to run one; open proposals of the kind stay as they are and can be withdrawn, rejected or run again after the switch is on again.
- qhost proposal commands status: show whether it is on; it needs no sudo for an administrator in the group qhost
The content is a JSON object with these fields and no others: commands, 1 to 10 commands, each one non-empty line of printable characters (no line break, tab or other control character) of at most 1000 characters, and reason, required, one line of at most 1000 characters. Example: {"commands": ["touch /srv/example/ready"], "reason": "the folder needs a marker file"}
Submit it like any other kind: qhost proposal submit --kind command --file <path>. qhost proposal show prints the commands and the reason word for word as text of the submitter, marked untrusted, with control characters replaced, and the fixed notice: unchecked, no computed effect, no undo. With --output json the text stays as submitted and the field content_untrusted is true. Neither qhost proposal approve nor sudo qhost host apply --proposal takes a proposal of this kind; they point to the command below.
sudo qhost proposal run <id> [--fingerprint <fingerprint>] [--confirm] [--timeout <duration>]: needs root and a person at a terminal, like qhost host apply --proposal: --agent and a process without a terminal are refused, and there is no --yes. It shows the commands, the reason and the fingerprint on standard error and, without --confirm, stops there with exit code 2 and runs nothing. With --confirm it asks y or n at the terminal, or takes --fingerprint, the fingerprint of the proposal as you reviewed it, and refuses a proposal with another fingerprint, a changed one and an expired one. Right before anything runs it reads the proposal again and takes the approval at the host service for the fingerprint that was shown. It then runs the commands one after the other with /bin/sh -c as root, each in a process group of its own, with the environment PATH, LANG=C and HOME=/root only, nothing taken from the caller, and standard input closed. Each command has a time limit, 5 minutes unless --timeout says otherwise, at most 1 hour; when it runs out the whole group is killed and the run counts as failed. The first command that does not exit with 0 ends the run, the commands after it do not run and the proposal ends as failed; otherwise it ends as applied. The report shows for each command the exit code, the output with standard output and standard error together cut at 64 KiB (the cut is marked) and cleared of known secrets, whether it ran out of time, or that it did not run; --output json gives it as a list. A run that did not succeed ends with exit code 1 after the report.
The run is a journal entry marked as not to be undone, with the commands, their exit codes and the human behind sudo; qhost host undo refuses it, and qhost host journal lists it as not reversible. qhost audit shows the entry with the proposal and the human, never the commands.
In the terminal interface the switch is the field Free proposals under Settings, General, which asks and runs sudo qhost proposal commands enable|disable after y. System, Proposals marks such a proposal as free commands in the warning colour. Its view shows the fixed notice, the reason as a quote and the commands numbered, word for word with control characters escaped; while the switch is off it offers no run and names the switch. A runs it: the question names the number of commands and then runs sudo qhost proposal run <id> --fingerprint <fingerprint> --confirm after y. The result lists each command with its exit code and output and the commands that did not run any more. System, Changes shows the entry as not to be undone.

Limits: at most 20 open proposals per submitter, at most 64 KiB per proposal, and an open proposal expires after 14 days.

States: open, withdrawn, rejected, approving, applied, failed and expired. An open proposal past its time shows as expired and cannot be approved. The fingerprint identifies exactly the content, and the service accepts an approval only with the fingerprint that was shown. Once accepted, the proposal is approving: it can no longer be withdrawn, rejected or approved again, and it ends as applied or as failed with a message. An approval that gets no result within 15 minutes, for example after an interrupted command, shows as failed; the submitter then submits a new proposal. The reason of a rejection and the message of a failure show in qhost proposal show.

Text of a submitter is shown with control characters replaced; --output json keeps the text as submitted.`

const hostAdminTopic = `The host commands read the local users, groups and folder permissions and change them through registered actions. Reading changes nothing and runs without sudo for root and for the administrators of the group qhost, everyone else gets one line and exit code 1. Changing needs root.

Reading:
  qhost host users
  qhost host user <name>
  qhost host groups
  qhost host group <name>
  qhost host folder <path>
  sudo qhost host file <path>

A user or group is a system user or group when its ID lies outside 1000 to 60000, so root and nobody count as system accounts. A user is an admin in the group sudo, a docker user in the group docker, and enabled in the group qhost. A group lists the users whose primary group it is as members.

Whether an account is locked or expired and which SSH keys it has are only readable as root. Otherwise they are unknown: absent in JSON and 'unknown' in the other formats. Keys are shown as type, comment and SHA256 fingerprint, never as the key itself.

host file reads as root and changes nothing: a folder is listed with name, kind (file, folder, link or other), size, owner, group, permissions and modification time of each entry, a link with its target as text. A file shows the same and its content; a file over 1 MiB or with binary content (a NUL byte or no valid UTF-8) shows only that, with too_large or binary set and an empty content. A link is never followed, neither on the way nor as the path itself.

A folder or file shows owner, group and the level of owner, group and others: nothing, read, or read and write. For a directory read and write both need the right to enter it; write without read counts as nothing. It also shows whether ACLs are set, the special bits setuid, setgid and sticky, and whether the path is protected: /, /etc, /usr, /boot, /proc, /sys and everything below the last five. Links and .. are resolved before that check.

Changing:
  sudo qhost host apply <action> [args...] --confirm
  sudo qhost host undo <journal-id> --confirm
  sudo qhost host journal

apply and undo ask no question. Without --confirm they show the sentence and the exact commands on standard error, change nothing, read no secret input, and exit with code 2; a secret input is shown as a placeholder. With --confirm they run. sudo qhost host apply --proposal <id> applies a proposal of the host instead of an action typed by hand, with the same journal, check, restore, protection rules and report; it is described in qhost help proposals. A change first records the previous state in the journal, then runs its commands, checks the result and, when a later command or the check fails, restores the previous state. A first command that fails changed nothing and is not taken back. The report shows the sentence, the exact commands, the check and the result applied, failed or restored, and a failed or restored change ends with exit code 1. A protection rule refuses a change before anything runs, shows the reason with the result refused and ends with exit code 1; it leaves no journal entry. Put -- before the action when an argument starts with a dash.

The journal lives in /var/lib/qhost-host/journal (folder 0700, files 0600, readable by root only), holds the latest 50 changes and, for each, what an undo would do. Secrets, passwords and key texts are read from standard input only, never from an argument, and appear nowhere in the report, the journal or the action log. Each change is reported to the host service, so qhost audit lists it with the action, the journal entry and the person behind sudo (SUDO_UID). When the host service is not running the change still stands, and the report warns that the audit entry is missing.

Actions (a secret input is marked with stdin):
  user.create <name> [admin] [docker] [group=<name>]...  (stdin: JSON {"password": "...", "keys": ["..."]}, keys optional)
  user.create-service <name> [home] [docker] [group=<name>]...
  user.set-password <name> [must-change]  (stdin: the new password)
  user.set-admin <name> on|off [without-qhost]
  user.set-docker <name> on|off
  user.enable <name>
  user.lock <name>, user.unlock <name>
  user.delete <name> [with-home]
  user.add-groups <name> <group>..., user.remove-groups <name> <group>...
  user.add-key <name>  (stdin: one public key line)
  user.remove-key <name> <SHA256 fingerprint>
  group.create <name>, group.delete <name>, group.rename <old> <new>
  group.set-members <group> +<user>|-<user>...
  folder.set-permissions <path> <owner> <group> <user-level> <group-level> <other-level> [recursive] [system-path]  (levels: nothing, read, read-write)
  file.write <path> [system-path]  (stdin: the content, text up to 1 MiB)
  file.create-folder <path> [system-path]
  file.rename <source> <target> [system-path]
  file.copy <source> <target> [permanent] [system-path]
  file.remove <path> [permanent] [system-path]

File actions: the path is absolute and clean. file.write writes an existing file in place, so its inode, owner, group, permissions and hard links stay, and creates a missing file in an existing folder with the owner and group of that folder; the content is never an argument and appears nowhere but in the journal. file.create-folder creates one folder in an existing folder the same way. No link is followed, on the way or at the end: a link in the path is refused. Nothing is written in a .git folder, in .qatlas/qhost of a stack folder or in /var/lib/qhost-host, which holds the journal of the host changes and is the way back; /proc and /sys are never written, and any other system path needs system-path. Content over 1 MiB or binary content is refused, and so is a file that already holds either. Undo puts the previous content, owner and permissions back, or removes what the change created, only as long as the file is unchanged since; a folder is removed only while empty. file.rename moves within one file system and mount, and the target must not exist; its undo moves it back while the target is the same inode and the source is free. file.copy and file.remove work on a file or a folder with everything below it. A copy keeps links as links, takes owner and group from the target folder and the mode of the source without setuid, setgid and sticky bit, appears only when complete, and refuses devices, FIFOs and sockets. The plan names the number of entries and the size. Up to 5000 entries and 10 MiB the undo of a copy removes it while it is unchanged, and the undo of a remove builds the tree again while the path is free, with content, links, owner, group, mode and time; hard links come back as separate files, ACLs and extended attributes do not. Larger trees are refused unless permanent is given: then the change is final, the journal keeps nothing, and there is no undo; more than 100000 entries are refused in any case. A folder that holds .git, .qatlas/qhost or the journal folder is not removed or renamed, and one that holds .git or .qatlas/qhost is not copied, copying out of one is allowed. A system folder such as /etc or /usr is never removed, renamed or taken as a target. The file actions cannot be proposed.

Protection rules: nobody takes administrator rights or access to qhost from themselves, locks or deletes their own account, and the last administrator stays; the groups sudo and qhost are neither renamed nor deleted; a user with running processes and a group that is the primary group of a user are not deleted; a system path is changed only with system-path and never together with everything below it. Lock is the normal way to stop someone: it blocks every login, also by SSH key, while running sessions stay open. Deleting a user keeps the home by default and can be undone, which creates the account again with the same UID, groups and password; with-home deletes the home and mail spool and cannot be undone.`

const hostTUITopic = `System shows the local users and groups of this host and the permissions of a folder on the sub-tabs Users, Groups and Folders, the proposals of administrators on Proposals, the latest changes of the host on Changes and the action log of the host service on Log. It reads the account files and the file system of the host, so it works without the deploy service and without sudo. What only root can read, the login state and the SSH keys of a user, shows as unknown unless the interface runs with sudo. Admin means the groups sudo and qhost together; an administrator without the group qhost shows as "yes, without qhost" and has no access to qhost yet. Docker means the group docker, which is practically root. System accounts and system groups, whose ID lies outside 1000 to 60000, are listed in a group of their own below the regular ones. r reads the users and groups again.

Every change shows a sentence in plain words, the exact commands dimmed below it, and y or n. y hands the terminal to sudo qhost host apply, which asks for the password itself; qhost never sees it. Started with sudo, the interface runs the command directly. A password or SSH key is typed into a hidden field and reaches the command on standard input only, never a command line or the screen. The result names the journal entry, and Changes takes the change back (see qhost help host-admin). The action log of the host service records every change with the person behind sudo, never with a password or key: Log and qhost audit list it, and qhost audit verify checks the chain of hashes that links its entries, where a broken chain means an entry was changed or removed.

Changes of root, of system accounts and of system groups ask in red and name the consequence. Only what cuts off the way back is refused: taking administrator rights from yourself, locking or deleting yourself, leaving no administrator with access to qhost, and deleting or renaming the groups sudo and qhost.

On Users, a creates a user with an initial password that must be changed at the first login, optionally as administrator, with Docker, in further groups and with SSH keys. e or enter offers the changes of the marked user, one at a time: access to qhost for an administrator without it, administrator on or off, Docker on or off, add to or remove from groups (several at once), a new password, add or remove an SSH key, lock and unlock. Lock is the normal way to stop someone: it blocks every login, also by SSH key, while running sessions stay open. x deletes the marked user after asking whether its home goes too: keeping the home is the default and can be undone, deleting the home and the mail spool cannot. A user with running processes is not deleted.

On Groups, a creates a group, e or enter adds and removes members of the marked group (several users at once with one confirmation; in sudo, adding also gives access to qhost and removing takes both), n renames it and x deletes it, each after a confirmation. A user whose primary group it is can neither be added nor removed, and such a group is not deleted. Deleting can be undone, which creates the group again with the same group number and members. Renaming always asks in red, because configurations such as sudoers address a group by its name and keep the old one; changes in the groups root and docker and in system groups ask in red too.

On Folders, enter asks for the absolute path of a folder, and tab completes it from the directories of the host. The page shows the owner, the group and what each of them and everyone else may do: nothing, read (open and list), or read and write; an ACL or a special bit (setuid, setgid, sticky) is named but not changed. e changes owner, group and the three levels, optionally for everything below the folder: the confirmation then names the number of entries, counted before anything runs, and a folder with more than 5000 entries or an entry that cannot be read is refused. A system path (/, /etc, /usr, /boot, /proc, /sys and everything below them) asks in red; /proc and /sys are never changed, and a system path itself is never changed together with everything below it. Undoing restores the owner, group and mode of every entry the change touched.

Proposals lists the proposals of changes to the host that administrators submitted, newest first, with their state. It reads them from the host service like Log, so it needs neither sudo nor the deploy service, and reads them again after a change and on r. enter shows a proposal: who proposed it, its state, the sentence in plain words, the exact commands dimmed as they would be typed by hand, and a proposed SSH key with its type, comment and fingerprint. The view is marked red where the change gives the Docker right, which means full control over the host, enables access to qhost, or touches root, a system account, a system group or a system path. A approves an open proposal with the same question as a change typed by hand, the sentence and the exact commands, and y runs sudo qhost host apply --proposal with the fingerprint that was shown: the root command reads the proposal again, checks the rules that apply to the host, makes the change with a journal entry that Changes can undo, and marks the proposal as applied. For a new user the interface asks for the initial password first, in a hidden field; it reaches the command on standard input only. N rejects an open proposal after a question and runs sudo qhost proposal reject. The notice at the start counts the open proposals of the host service and of the deploy service; one that cannot be reached counts for none.

Changes lists the latest 50 changes of the host from the journal, newest first, with time, the person behind sudo, the sentence, the result and whether an undo is possible; below the list stand the sentence and the exact commands of the undo of the marked change. Only root can read the journal: started with sudo the interface reads it on its own, otherwise r reads it through sudo qhost host journal, so no password prompt appears unasked. enter on a change asks to undo it with the same question as a change, the sentence and the exact commands dimmed below it, and y runs sudo qhost host undo. An undo cannot itself be undone, so its question says so and its yes is red. A change that cannot be undone, such as a deleted home, one that did not stand and one undone already say why instead of asking. After a change made from the interface the page says that the journal is older than the host until r reads it again.

Log shows the latest 200 entries of the action log of the host service, newest first, with time, user, action, result and the change of the host with its journal entry, under the result of qhost audit verify: intact with the number of days and entries checked, or broken with the day, the line and the kind of damage. Every request to the host service is an entry of this log itself, so the page reads it when it opens for the first time, after a change made from the interface and on r, never on a timer. qhost audit filters it further.`

const troubleshootingTopic = `Every file qhost stores and reads back carries a format number. A file whose number is missing, newer than this version reads or older than it can convert is not read. qhost does not guess at it, does not fill in defaults and never overwrites, moves or deletes it; pruning and retention leave it alone too. The message names the file and the numbers, for example "<file>: format number 2 is newer than the 1 this version reads; the file is left unchanged".

What happens depends on the file. If it belongs to the core state of a service, that service does not start. It writes the message to its log, and qhost status shows the service as not running and names the file. As root, qhost status checks the state files of a service that does not run; without root it says so and points to sudo qhost status. If the file is only one part, the service keeps running and the part is shown as not readable with its file: a proposal in qhost proposal list, the settings of a stack as the finding unreadable, a backup as unreadable in qhost stack backup list, and the log of a run in qhost stack runs (qhost stack log names the file).

Next step: a number that is newer means a newer qhost wrote the file, so install that version again (qhost update status shows what is installed). A missing or too old number means the file comes from an earlier release: move it aside, for example with mv, and start the service again, which then begins that part afresh; keep the moved file as long as you may need it. Edit the number in the file only if you know what the newer fields mean. Then start the service again with systemctl start qhost-deploy, or qhost-deploy@<user> for an instance. For the webhook receiver, qhost status shows hook_config as unreadable with its file.`
